# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET streaming extractor integration built on the Intropy
framework, for files too large to ride the message broker. One run = one
sweep: list the files under the incoming prefix, stream each record-by-record
in constant memory through its registered streamed extractor, write the
transformed output to storage, publish one claim-check CloudEvent per file (a
reference to the output, never the payload), archive the consumed input, and
exit. Scheduling lives outside the block — activation cadence is deployment
configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob in production); locally the system host
runs the block once at startup.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10
- App type: Run-to-completion console job (no web endpoints; one sweep per
  run, then exit). Hosted by the framework's `RunToCompletionRunner`, which
  owns the Dapr sidecar lifecycle, tracing, and the exit-code contract: `0`
  success / empty prefix / cancelled, `1` job failure, `2` infrastructure
  failure (sidecar unavailable).
- Runner: the framework's `RunToCompletionRunner` (in
  `Intropy.Framework.Hosting`), driven by `{{ .name }}.StreamJob` — an
  `IRunToCompletionJob` that adapts the sidecar-free `Sweep` and reports its
  `JobRunSummary`.
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Source port: `{{ .port }}` — the named interface the extractor reads
  the outside world through; the system host declares it in `Ports.cs`
  and the Dapr binding uses the same name, resolved to a local folder in
  development. In production the same name resolves to the environment's
  binding from the deploy overlay's GitOps values — rebinding
  the port is a deployment change, never a code change
- Pub/sub component: `pubsub`
- Topic: `{{ .topic }}` — the extractor's half of the system contract; the
  consuming component subscribes to the same topic
- CloudEvent envelope: source `{{ if .eventSource }}{{ .eventSource }}{{ else }}{{ .eventSourceValue }}{{ end }}`, type `{{ if .eventType }}{{ .eventType }}{{ else }}{{ .eventTypeValue }}{{ end }}`
- Idempotency Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Business Incident Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Shared contracts: the published contract (`{{ .contract }}` — a claim-check
  reference: output location, file name, source file name, entity type,
  record/reject counts) lives in the sibling project `../{{ .contracts }}`
  (namespace `{{ .contracts }}`), referenced from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`.
  The sibling is scaffolded by this template's dependency declaration; when
  it already exists (another component created it) the scaffold leaves it
  untouched. The per-record shapes (`Source{{ .contract }}Record`,
  `{{ .contract }}Record`) stay local in `src/Model/`.

## Important files

- `Taskfile.yml` - build and test tasks (`task build`, `task test`; the
  integration suite also runs separately via `task test:integration`).
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage image build on the .NET chiseled runtime
  (`docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .` —
  the shared contracts sibling enters as a named context); the image contains
  only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components come from the deployment
  environment.
- `src/Program.cs` - entrypoint: builds the service provider via `Composition`
  and runs the framework `RunToCompletionRunner`.
- `src/Composition/Composition.cs` - every service registration: the keyed
  source adapter (`IFileStreamAdapter`), the streamed extractors (one
  `AddStreamedExtractor` per entity type), the publish edge (the sender is a
  DI-registered `SendStep<TCtx>` — a `DaprTopicPublisher` in production), and
  the `Sweep`. Note the namespace/type collision: `{{ .name }}.Composition`
  is both namespace and class, so call sites inside nested namespaces use the
  fully qualified `Composition.Composition.*`.
- `src/Sweep.cs` - the sweep: lists the incoming prefix, matches each file to
  a streamed extractor by entity type, builds the file's `StreamSource` from
  the adapter, runs the extraction, publishes the reference, archives the
  input, and routes a business incident for any file it cannot process.
- `src/StreamJob.cs` - the runner adapter over the sweep.
- `src/{{ .contract }}Context.cs` - the sweep's per-run context: carries the
  streamed outcome of the file currently being published.
- `src/Process/ReferencePublishStep.cs` - builds the one claim-check
  CloudEvent per file (subject/id = source file name; data = the reference).
- `src/Process/Streamed/` - the sample streamed extractor's steps
  (`Deserializer` locates the record array in the source envelope;
  `{{ .contract }}ProcessStep` is the fused validate+transform). Duplicate
  per entity type.
- `src/Constants.cs` - component/topic names, keyed-adapter key, CloudEvent
  envelope values, the sample entity type (root namespace — consumed by both
  `src` and the tests).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/` - sweep-run and composition tests
  against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes (`task test:integration`;
  `task coverage` reports branch coverage).

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json` (copied to the build output),
overridden by `appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables —
`Composition` builds `IConfiguration` from the binary directory, registers it
in DI, and reads the platform-service app ids from `PlatformServices:*`.
File layout (incoming/output/processed prefixes) binds to
`src/Configuration/IngestOptions.cs` from the `Ingest` section. Deployment
overlays the file via ConfigMap.

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this extractor runs via its system host,
which runs it once at startup and provides every Dapr component (source
binding, pub/sub, platform services). A re-run is a host restart. The
component-level loop is build and test:

```bash
task build
task test
```

The integration suite resolves the sweep exactly as production composition
registers it, fakes only the edges (`InMemoryFileAdapter` — which implements
the streaming `IFileStreamAdapter` contract — `FakeTopic`,
`FakeIdempotencyServiceClient`, `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient` — from
`Intropy.Framework.Testing`), and asserts on fake state. `task coverage`
runs the integration suite with branch coverage (Cobertura report under the
integration project's `TestResults/`).

## Development notes

- The source adapter is registered as a keyed singleton
  (`Constants.SourceFileAdapterKey`) against `IFileStreamAdapter` — the
  streaming extension of `IFileAdapter` (open read/write streams, exists,
  move). Inject it via `[FromKeyedServices(...)]` or resolve via
  `GetRequiredKeyedService<IFileStreamAdapter>(...)`. The default
  registration is the zero-dependency `LocalFileStreamAdapter`; production
  swaps in the SDK adapter (e.g. `Intropy.Framework.Adapters.Azure`'s
  `AzureBlobStorageStreamAdapter`) at this one registration — credentials
  from configuration, never hard-coded. Dapr bindings are not an option for
  large files: they buffer the payload through the sidecar, which defeats
  constant-memory streaming.
- The claim-check contract: the event carries a reference to the streamed
  output, never the payload. The consuming component resolves the reference
  by streaming the named file from the same storage behind the source port —
  the file-adapter kind and the prefix layout are part of the system
  contract, on equal footing with the topic and the contract record.
- Sweep idempotency is the archive's contract: a processed file moves to the
  processed prefix, so the next sweep never re-reads it. Keep the reference
  event's id/subject stable (the source file name) so consumers can
  deduplicate a re-delivery after a publish-then-archive crash.
- One streamed extractor per entity type: duplicate the
  `AddStreamedExtractor` registration and the `src/Process/Streamed/` steps
  per entity. A file whose entity type has no registered extractor routes a
  business incident and is left in place; the rest of the sweep continues.
- A zero-valid-record file produces no output and no event — the input is
  archived silently. A streamed failure or a publish failure leaves the file
  under the incoming prefix for the next scheduled run.
{{- if .empty }}
- The entity mapping, deserializer, process step, and reference construction
  are scaffolded empty (`NotImplementedException`). The sweep wiring
  (extraction, publish, archive, business-incident routing) stays in place.
  When you implement: the entity mapping defines the producer's file→entity
  rule; keep the reference event's id/subject stable (the source file name).
- The integration suite is real and fails out of the box by design — `task
  test` is red until the steps land (only the empty-prefix and composition
  tests start green). The pinned behaviors flip green one by one as the
  implementation progresses; no test edits are needed to get there.
{{- end }}

## Framework guidance

How-to knowledge for the Intropy framework (pipelines, blocks, endpoints, Dapr components, testing) lives in the Intropy skills collection under `.agents/skills/`. If it is not installed, run `intropy skills collection add --name intropy --ref harbor.intropy.io/skills/index:latest`.
